# Citation metadata for SpearShift (Citation File Format 1.2.0; GitHub and Zenodo read this file).
# The DOI below is a placeholder until the Zenodo deposit of the v1.0.0-thesis archive mints one.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SpearShift, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "SpearShift: an evaluation framework for open-weight LLM detectors under AI-rewritten spear-phishing"
authors:
  - family-names: Mashan
    given-names: Bakhit Ali
version: v1.0.0-thesis
date-released: 2026-11-01
repository-code: "https://github.com/aliholmes5544/LLM"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX   # placeholder: replace with the DOI minted by Zenodo for the v1.0.0-thesis deposit
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
keywords:
  - spear-phishing
  - phishing detection
  - large language models
  - open-weight models
  - robustness
  - paraphrase
  - obfuscation
  - evaluation framework
  - reproducibility
abstract: >-
  SpearShift is the reproducible evaluation framework of the Master's thesis "LLM vs. LLM: Evaluating the
  Effectiveness of Open-Source Language Models in Detecting AI-Generated Spear-Phishing Emails". It builds a
  benchmark from published corpora (LLM-generated spear-phishing e-mails of Nahmias et al., 2024; Enron and
  SpamAssassin legitimate mail; the English part of E-PhishLLM, Pajola et al., 2025), derives paraphrased and
  obfuscated variants under meaning checks, runs six open-weight detectors (GGUF Q4_K_M, llama.cpp) under four
  prompting strategies against three supervised baselines, and scores them with cluster-bootstrap confidence
  intervals, paired exact McNemar tests and Holm correction within pre-registered families. Licences: the code
  is MIT; the derived variant dataset and the generation cache are CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, the licence of the Nahmias
  et al. (2024) data they derive from; the E-PhishLLM source carries no licence and is downloaded by script,
  never redistributed.
